  "tldr": "One response-adaptive experiment must serve a continuum of quantiles whose Neyman-optimal allocations generally disagree. The focal object is the minimax function \\(\\Phi(r)\\), which records the smallest maximal LIL-normalized width of an anytime-valid finite-population QTE band attainable at whole-curve Neyman regret at most \\(r\\). The formal program derives the threshold-score martingale and its random quadratic-variation loss, proves exact time-and-quantile coverage and a functional LIL lower bound, and asks for a primal--dual predictable design with stitched CDF inversion that attains every exposed frontier point.",
  "project_justification": {
    "gap": "The literature separately gives fixed-horizon quantile-specific adaptive efficiency, scalar ATE Neyman regret, and i.i.d. time-uniform quantile bands. It does not characterize the experiment-level conflict created by a continuum of incompatible QTE allocation targets.",
    "niche": "The missing object is a design-based Pareto frontier whose two coordinates are both functions of the same threshold-indexed predictable quadratic variation.",
    "fill": "The project defines that frontier over known predictable designs and honest bands, supplies its functional LIL converse, and seeks an attaining online saddle procedure with an observable stitched-inversion band."
  },
  "related_work": "Li, Luo, Wang, and Feng motivate quantile-targeted response adaptation and derive fixed-horizon efficiency for a selected QTE; their allocation calculation supplies the local loss but not a whole-curve or anytime criterion. Dai, Gradu, and Harshaw introduce design-based Neyman regret for the ATE, and Noarov, Fogliato, Bertran, and Roth obtain an anytime near-logarithmic scalar guarantee through strong convexity. Howard and Ramdas derive the sharp i.i.d. empirical-process LIL and quantile inversion, while Mineiro and Howard construct always-valid, sometimes-trivial counterfactual CDF bands under nonstationarity. Zhang and Zheng provide fixed-horizon QTE-process linearization under covariate-adaptive randomization. Simchi-Levi and Wang formulate a welfare-versus-mean-inference Pareto problem, but their error coordinate is scalar and not an anytime QTE band. Cook, Mishler, and Ramdas and Hadad, Hirshberg, Zhan, Wager, and Athey supply adaptive martingale weighting for mean effects. Bibaut and Kallus survey why adaptive inference requires reweighting, always-valid Gaussian approximations, or direct inversion. These papers inform separate steps; none is treated as a theorem-level comparator that the stated results automatically tighten.",
  "technical_internal_limitation": "The regular core excludes atoms and vanishing-density cusps. Extending \\(\\Phi\\) to inverse-modulus widths is a distinct mixed-regime problem because an LIL-sized CDF band need not map to a shrinking outcome-scale QTE band.",
